---
/**
 * The open-source explainer.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS RATHER THAN A BARE LINK. The header's "Fork this site"
 * action used to point straight at GitHub. A repository landing page answers
 * "what is this" badly for someone who has not already decided they want it —
 * it opens on a file tree, and the README's first screen is a premise and a
 * table of rules. This page gives the one-paragraph answer first, and keeps
 * the link to the repository where someone who wants it cannot miss it.
 *
 * Plain language is the constraint here, deliberately. AGENTS.md and DESIGN.md
 * are written for whoever edits the codebase; this is written for someone
 * deciding whether to bother. No jargon survives unless it is defined on
 * first use.
 *
 * CloseBot is disclosed in the prose, per AGENTS.md — the recommendation below
 * is a founder recommending his own product, and an open-source page is
 * exactly where that would otherwise read as undisclosed promotion.
 */
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { person } from '../data/person';
import { SOURCE_REPO_URL } from '../data/site';
---

<PageLayout
  seo={{
    title: 'Open Source: Fork This Site',
    description:
      'The code behind this site is public and MIT licensed. An experiment in maintaining an SEO-sound site almost entirely through Claude Code, free to fork.',
  }}
  heading="Open source"
  lede="The code behind this site is public. Take it, change the name, and it is yours."
  width="wide"
>
  <div class="lg:grid lg:grid-cols-[14rem_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-x-16">
    <div class="mb-10 lg:mb-0">
      {/*
        The repository link, pulled out of the prose into the position the
        portrait occupies on /about/. Someone who arrived here from the header
        button already knows what they want; they should not have to read an
        essay to find it.
      */}
      <a
        href={SOURCE_REPO_URL}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
        class="inline-block border border-line px-4 py-2 font-mono text-kicker tracking-[0.08em] text-ink uppercase no-underline shadow-[3px_3px_0_var(--color-ink)] transition-colors duration-100 hover:border-accent hover:text-accent-hover"
      >
        View on GitHub<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
      </a>

      <dl class="mt-8 space-y-4">
        <div>
          <dt class="kicker">Licence</dt>
          <dd class="text-small mt-1 text-ink">MIT, for the code</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="kicker">Built with</dt>
          <dd class="text-small mt-1 text-ink">Astro, Cloudflare Workers</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="kicker">Cost to run</dt>
          <dd class="text-small mt-1 text-ink">A domain, and nothing else</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          {/*
            The space before an inline link is written as {' '} on purpose.
            A newline between text and an <a> collapses to nothing here, which
            renders as "throughClaude Code" -- invisible in source, obvious on
            the page, and easy to reintroduce when rewrapping a paragraph.
          */}
          This site is an experiment: how far can you get maintaining a
          genuinely SEO-sound personal site almost entirely through{' '}
          <a href="https://claude.com/claude-code" rel="noopener">Claude Code</a>, an
          AI coding agent that works in your terminal? Not generating pages and
          hoping, but holding a real standard — one that fails the build when it
          slips.
        </p>

        <p>
          The answer so far is: further than I expected, and only because the
          standard is written down and machine-checked. Everything I learned
          doing it is in the repository, including the parts that did not work.
        </p>

        <h2>Why I made it public</h2>

        <p>
          Most advice about SEO is someone describing what they think works.
          This is the whole thing — the rules, the tests that enforce them, and
          a site you can look at. You can disagree with it concretely instead of
          in the abstract.
        </p>

        <p>
          It is also a usable starting point. If you want a personal site that
          is fast, accessible and correctly structured, and you would rather not
          assemble that yourself, this one already is. Replace my name and my
          writing and you are done.
        </p>

        <h2>How it works, in plain terms</h2>

        <p>
          Every page is built once, ahead of time, into plain HTML files. There
          is no server deciding anything when someone visits — the file already
          exists and gets handed over. That is why it is fast, why it costs
          nothing to host, and why there is very little that can break.
        </p>

        <p>
          The unusual part is that the rules are enforced rather than
          documented. A page cannot be published without a title, a description,
          and the structured data search engines read. Not "should not" —
          cannot. The build refuses it. There are a few hundred automated checks
          covering broken links, heading order, missing image descriptions,
          pages nothing links to, and more.
        </p>

        <p>
          That matters most when an AI agent is doing the work. An agent will
          cheerfully write a page with a missing description, and nobody would
          notice for months. Here, it fails immediately and says why. The rules
          are the thing that makes the automation trustworthy, not the other way
          round.
        </p>

        <h2>Add a chat widget and analytics, free</h2>

        <p>
          {/*
            Disclosure first, in the prose. AGENTS.md: the relationship is said
            in the piece, not inherited from the header.
          */}
          One setting is worth knowing about, and I should say plainly that I
          co-founded the company behind it. Drop a free{' '}
          <a href={person.worksFor.url} rel="noopener">{person.worksFor.name}</a>{' '}
          Sales Pixel into one line of configuration and the site gets a chat
          widget that answers questions, plus visitor numbers — page views and
          sessions — without adding a separate analytics service.
        </p>

        <p>
          You do not need it. Leave the setting empty and no widget appears;
          everything else works exactly the same. But it is free, it is one
          line, and it covers two things most people end up bolting on
          separately.
        </p>

        <h2>What you get, and what stays mine</h2>

        <p>
          The code is MIT licensed: use it, change it, deploy it, build
          something you sell. No attribution required beyond keeping the licence
          file, and nothing to send back.
        </p>

        <p>
          My writing, my photograph and the share images with my name on them
          are not included — a fork replaces those anyway, and the repository
          lists exactly what to swap. The point is that you get the machinery,
          not my identity.
        </p>

        <h2>Starting from it</h2>

        <p>
          Clone it, run two commands, and it is on your machine. The README has
          a section written specifically for AI coding agents, so if you point
          one at the project, the first thing it reads is the rulebook rather
          than guessing at the conventions.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you build something with it, I would genuinely like to see it:
          <a href={`mailto:${person.email}`}>{person.email}</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
